==================================================================================
  MODEL COMPARISON  |  8 berths, varied demand ratios, seed=42
==================================================================================
  Model                            Revenue   vs Basic   Assigned  Status  Scenario
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  1. Basic                          11,977      +0.0%     7/8     FAIL    1x demand, baseline
  2. + Compat + Power                8,242     -31.2%     4/8     OK      1x demand, cost of correctness
  3. + Side-by-side                 22,061     +84.2%    10/24    OK      3x demand, pack more boats
  4. Temporal stable                61,768    +415.7%     5/8     FAIL    1x demand, 14-day season
  5. Temporal + Compat + Power      61,755    +415.6%     5/8     OK      1x demand, realistic temporal
  6. + Gap-fill discount 20%       219,923   +1736.2%    18/24    OK      long+short stays, fill empty days
==================================================================================

  What each row tells you:

  Row 1 vs Row 2  (Basic -> Compat+Power, same 1x instance)
    Revenue change: -3,735  (-31.2%)
    The solver loses revenue by respecting real-world rules (boat-type
    compatibility, power capacity). This is the cost of correctness.

  Row 3 alone  (Side-by-side at 3x demand)
    Revenue: 22,061  vs Row 2 basic-constrained at 1x: 8,242
    Side-by-side is compared at 3x demand because that is where it matters.
    When berths are scarce, fitting two boats into one wide berth adds revenue.
    At 1x demand (Row 1-2) there are empty berths for every boat, so sharing
    adds nothing.

  Row 1 vs Row 4  (Basic static -> Temporal, same 1x boats)
    Revenue change: +49,791  (415.7%)
    Moving to a 14-day season lets each berth serve sequential boats.
    Berth occupied days 0-5 earns from boat A; days 6-13 from boat B.

  Row 4 vs Row 5  (Temporal stable -> + Compat + Power)
    Revenue change: -13  (-0.0%)
    Adding constraints to the temporal model removes (berth,boat,day)
    combinations that break real-world rules. This is the recommended
    production baseline.

  Row 5 vs Row 6  (Temporal -> + Gap discount, different instance)
    Revenue change: +158,168
    Row 6 uses a different instance (long-stay + short-stay boats).
    The 20% discount attracts short-stay boats to fill empty berth-days.
    Every discounted day that was previously empty adds net positive revenue.

==================================================================================
  DEMAND/SUPPLY RATIO SWEEP  (Basic vs Side-by-side vs Temporal)
  Fixed: 8 berths, seed=42. Boats: 4, 8, 16, 24, 32 (0.5x to 4x)
==================================================================================
   Ratio   Boats       Basic       + SbS   SbS lift    Temporal   Temp lift
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    0.5x       4       4,853       5,139        +6%      19,867       +309%
    1.0x       8      11,977      13,397       +12%      61,768       +416%
    2.0x      16      20,187      26,506       +31%      90,285       +347%
    3.0x      24      20,489      29,700       +45%     107,286       +424%
    4.0x      32      20,591      30,418       +48%     118,753       +477%
==================================================================================

  Key insights from the sweep:
  - Basic revenue plateaus once berths are full (around 1.5x demand).
    Adding more boats has no effect on the static model.
  - Side-by-side lift grows with competition: +6% at 0.5x, up to +48% at 4x.
    This confirms that Row 3 of the main table needs 3x demand to be meaningful.
  - Temporal lift is large at every ratio (multi-day season multiplies revenue)
    and also grows as more boats compete for the same slots.

